# Expectation record for 13_floating_point/002_float_int_conversions.c
#
# Reproduce one cell by hand with no harness: render the three command templates
# below, substituting the target triple, the optimization level, the source path,
# the output path, and the target's runner (empty on the natively executing target).

program            = 002_float_int_conversions
area               = 13_floating_point
description        = Conversions in both directions between the floating and integer types, at representable boundaries and with truncation toward zero, in a folded and a volatile-runtime variant, plus four round trips
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
Free of undefined and unspecified behaviour by construction, and the conversion rules
are the whole subject here, so they are argued precisely:
- FLOATING TO INTEGER. C11 6.3.1.4p1 discards the fractional part and leaves the
  behaviour undefined only when THE INTEGRAL PART cannot be represented in the
  destination. Every such conversion below is inside its destination by a wide margin --
  the narrowest margin anywhere is the 5 that separates 250 from unsigned char's maximum,
  and the narrowest on a signed destination is the 27 that separates 100 from signed
  char's. The two negative floating sources, -1000000.5 and -0.75, are converted ONLY to
  signed destinations, where their integral parts -1000000 and 0 are both comfortably in
  range. No negative floating value is converted to any unsigned type anywhere, which is
  a requirement of this program rather than a tidiness.
- INTEGER TO FLOATING. An integer not exactly representable in the destination format is
  converted with implementation-defined rounding, so every integer converted below is
  EXACTLY representable in the format it is converted to and no rounding decision is ever
  taken. binary32 carries 24 significand bits and binary64 carries 53; the exactness of a
  value depends on the width of its ODD PART, since trailing factors of two are absorbed
  by the exponent. 4000000000 is 2^11 * 5^9 and needs 21 bits; 4000000000000 is
  2^14 * 5^12 and needs the 28 bits of 5^12 = 244140625; 1234567890123 is odd and needs
  41. The last two therefore exceed binary32's 24 bits and are converted only to double,
  never to float.
- No signed integer overflow, no division by zero and no shift operator, so no shift
  count can be out of range.
- No array indexing beyond bounds, no pointer arithmetic, no aliasing violation, and no
  floating object inspected byte-wise or reinterpreted through overlapping storage.
- No read of uninitialized storage, and no object modified twice between sequence points.
- Nothing depends on unspecified evaluation order: each volatile operand is read exactly
  once, in a statement of its own, and no argument list contains more than one
  side-effecting argument.
- Hermetic, as constraint C4 requires: every input is a literal in the source, nothing is
  opened or allocated, and no environment variable or program argument is read.
- main returns 0, inside the 0-125 range the suite requires.
- Verified empirically on this branch: the default strict warning gate produces zero
  diagnostics at -O0, -O1 and -O2, and the sanitizer gate runs to completion with empty
  stderr and exit status 0.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
FLOATING-POINT DETERMINISM rests on three deliberate choices, each verified by
measurement rather than assumed:
1. Every floating value in the program is a DYADIC RATIONAL -- an integer, or an integer
   plus a sum of negative powers of two -- so it is exactly representable in binary32 and
   binary64 alike and no rounding decision is ever taken.
2. Every printed floating value carries an explicit precision with room to spare, so no
   default precision is relied on and no digit is at the mercy of a rounding mode.
3. Excess intermediate precision cannot perturb an exact value. This matters because i686
   evaluates floating expressions in x87 extended precision (FLT_EVAL_METHOD 2) while the
   other three targets do not; the exactness of every operand and every result is what
   makes the difference unobservable.
No not-a-number, no infinity, no negative zero, no subnormal and no division by zero is
produced or assumed anywhere.

Truncation is toward zero rather than toward negative infinity, and the two negative cases
pin that down: -1000000.5 gives -1000000 rather than -1000001, and -0.75 gives 0 rather
than -1. A backend that rounded instead of truncating, or truncated the wrong way, changes
those two fields and nothing else, which is what localises the defect.

THE TWO-VARIANT RULE. Every conversion is performed twice, once over constants and once
over volatile-qualified operands, so a divergence is attributable to the folder or to the
backend rather than to "one of the two".

Plain char signedness is implementation-defined and was measured as signed on x86_64 and
i686 and unsigned on aarch64 and riscv64; no plain-char value is printed. sizeof(long)
and sizeof(void *) were measured as 4 on i686 and 8 on the other three targets
(docs/technical-specifications.md lines 457-462); no long, size_t, ptrdiff_t, intptr_t or
pointer value is printed and no long or pointer conversion specification is used. Only
int, long long and their unsigned counterparts appear as integer destinations, each the
same width on all four targets. No address or pointer value is printed. The two character conversions name signed char and unsigned char
explicitly for that reason.

long double is absent, its representation having been measured at 16, 12, 16 and 16 bytes
across the four targets. It belongs to 004_long_double_target_restricted in this area,
which carries NO marker: that program's record instead disables oracle (b) alone with the
measured representation difference as its recorded reason, and keeps oracles (a) and (c)
enabled on all four targets at all three optimization levels.

No target is dropped, no oracle is disabled here and this record carries no
expected-divergence marker: a divergence is a genuine finding. Measured on this branch with gcc 13.4.0 and the matching i686, aarch64 and riscv64 cross
drivers, executed natively and under QEMU user-mode 10.1.0: stdout is byte-identical and
the exit status is 0 in all twelve target-and-optimization-level cells.
END
